# SYNOPSIS

```usage
cat [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`cat` reads each <file> in order and writes it on standard output.
With no <file> it reads standard input to its end.
Data is copied as bytes, never checked or changed.
Output goes out a line at a time, and in pieces when a line is long.

Each <file> is opened in the namespace `cat` was started with, and must be an absolute path.
A <file> that cannot be opened or read is reported and the next is read.

`cat` takes no flags: an argument beginning with `-` is a file's name, and `-` does not mean standard input.

In the POSIX namespace `cat` is sbase's command instead (see {The POSIX command|#posix}).

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% cat /proc/1/status
pid=1 name=svcd state=waiting threads=1 mem=30224K sid=1
% cat /n/u9fs/nothing
cat: /n/u9fs/nothing: file does not exist
```

# SEE ALSO

ls(1), tail(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <file> that fails is reported on standard error, as `cat: /n/u9fs/nothing: file does not exist`.
If any <file> fails the exit string is `error`.
Reading standard input, the exit string is `read error` when a read fails; it is `no namespace` when `cat` was started with <file> arguments and no namespace.

# BUGS

A relative path is refused as `bad message`, the error a malformed path is given.

Beware of `cat a b >a` and `cat a b >b`, which destroy input files before reading them.

@node=posix title="The POSIX command"

```text
cat [-u] [file ...]
```

In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)), `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so `cat` runs sbase's command, as POSIX specifies.
It reads each <file> in order and writes it on standard output; with no <file>, or for a <file> of `-`, it reads standard input.
A <file> that cannot be opened is reported and skipped; a failed write ends it.

: `-u`
  Accepted and ignored: output is never buffered beyond each read.

sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/cat.1` in the repository.
